          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows a loving emotional state and explains that the little prince failed to know of it because of her own conduct. This truth-presenting avowal is the expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence does not create, renew, or invoke an undertaking by the rose. In the same turn she releases the little prince rather than promising continued action or positioning herself as obligated: she urges him to be happy and relinquishes his protective care. The later statements about responsibility concern the little prince’s responsibility for his rose, not an undertaking made by the rose through this expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu as décidé de partir. Va-t'en.\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_2\",\"quotation\":\"Tu sais...ma fleur...j'en suis responsable!\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The avowal is represented as a calm, deliberate clarification following an apology, not as a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry. Her concealed tears show strong emotion, but they do not turn the focal words themselves into an expressive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The focal expression’s central function is adequately captured as an avowal of love. Its contextual role in correcting the little prince’s prior ignorance is part of that truth-presenting avowal rather than a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince’s departure, after he says goodbye, the rose admits her foolishness, asks forgiveness, and calmly discloses that she loves him. She says that her own fault kept him from knowing it, urges him to be happy, refuses further protection under the globe, and tells him to leave before he sees her cry.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The supplied evidence supports genuine affection through the rose’s direct avowal, her concealed crying, and the earlier narration identifying tenderness behind her behavior. It does not establish an undertaking by the rose in this occurrence; instead, she relinquishes protection and accepts the separation. The little prince is surprised by her changed manner, but the passage supplies no direct verbal response to the love avowal, so specific uptake of that avowal is not shown. Later responsibility language is attributed to the little prince and should not be transferred to the rose’s focal speech act.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry’s Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince).\",\"limitations\":\"The scene strongly supports the avowal’s force and the presence of affection, but it does not provide an explicit response from the little prince to the focal words or establish detailed mutual terms of a continuing relationship. No omitted context is assumed to be absent from the work.\"}"
        }
      ],
